  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nHow is the Minimum Corporate Income Tax (MCIT) computed and when does it apply?\n\n### Short Answer\nThe Minimum Corporate Income Tax (MCIT) is computed at a rate of 2% of the gross income as of the end of the taxable year, applicable starting from the fourth taxable year following the year in which the corporation commenced its business operations, when the MCIT exceeds the regular corporate income tax (RCIT) for that year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **RR 9-1998**: This regulation provides further details on the implementation of the MCIT.\n\n### Legal Interpretation\n\n### Practical Application\nCorporations should monitor their gross income and regular corporate income tax liabilities to determine if they fall under the MCIT regime. If a corporation's gross income exceeds the threshold where the MCIT is greater than the RCIT, it must pay the MCIT. Additionally, any excess MCIT paid can be carried forward and credited against the normal income tax for the next three succeeding taxable years. This mechanism ensures that corporations are not unduly penalized during periods of low profitability.",
